{"records":[{"id":"gmg54ngfnk","title":"Trends in Non-squamous cervical cancer incidence, histological type, stage, age, and human papillomavirus type in southwest China from 2009 to 2021: A Large Retrospective Study","description":"Nowadays, with the declining incidence of squamous cell cancer, the incidence of non-squamous cervical cancer is rising in developed countries. However, little is known about non-squamous cervical cancer in China. In this study, we retrospectively analyzed the pattern of non-squamous cervical cancer (incidence, age, histological type, stage, and human papillomavirus subtype) in southwestern China between January 1, 2009 and December 31, 2021. The histological classification was conducted according to World Health Organization (WHO) 2020 tumor classification. HPV detection for paraffin-embedded tumor tissue was done by use PCR-reverse dot blot hybridization probe assay. In our study, we found that the trends of increasing non-squamous cervical cancer incidence existed in cervical cancer in the older population, and most cases were associated with HPV. The rate of HPV-independent cervical cancer was small, but the majority of patients were diagnosed at an advanced stage. In contrast to the increasing incidence trends of adenocarcinoma for young women in some western countries, our study showed an increasing age. These results call for increasing effort in screening and HPV vaccination in China.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-05T09:13:30.968Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gmg54ngfnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gmg54ngfnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Liang","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Epidemiological Research"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gnp8m7wk6h","title":"Transcriptomic Profiling of Select Genes Implicated in Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia among Individuals of Reproductive Age in Ibadan, Nigeria","description":"Cervical cancer remains a leading cause of cancer-related mortality among women in Nigeria, primarily due to late detection. Genetic alterations in host genes contribute significantly to its pathogenesis. This study aims to validate the differential expression, mutational status, and clinical significance of SPP1, ANXA1, ANXA3, TMEM45A, IL-18, IGHG2, KRT10, CD177, ERO1α, ANKRD31, GSTA4, and MREG genes in patients presenting with symptoms of cervical cancer. A total of twenty-four (24) consenting female patients were recruited for this study; twenty-two (22) of them were those presenting with symptoms of cervical cancer, and the remaining two (2) were those who were clinically and histologically confirmed not to have any symptoms of cervical cancer. Tissue samples were obtained surgically and examined histopathologically. Total RNA was isolated, and gene expression levels were quantified using RT-qPCR. Gene expression across normal, tumour, and metastatic tissues, and their association with cancer hallmarks, were analysed using TNMPlot, muTarget, and Cancer Hallmark Enrichment Plot. Gene ontology and functional enrichment analyses were also performed. Histopathological analysis identified eight morphological categories: normal control (NC), mild infiltration/inflammation (MI), low-grade (LSIL) and high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesions (HSIL), chronic cervicitis (CC), well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma (WISCC), moderately-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma (MSCC), and poorly-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma (PISCC). Most of the genes were upregulated in MI, LSIL, MSCC, HSIL, WISCC, and CC, indicating roles in proliferation, immune evasion, and metastasis. Notably, ERO1α, IGHG2, IL-18, TMEM45A, GSTA4, ANXA1, and ANXA3 were downregulated in early-stage lesions, supporting their potential as early diagnostic biomarkers. Collectively, the present study findings highlight the studied genes as key contributors to cervical cancer progression and underscore their potential utility as biomarkers for early detection and targeted therapy in Nigerian women.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-23T18:38:59.728Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gnp8m7wk6h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gnp8m7wk6h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Saviour","last_name":"Usin","orcid":"0000-0003-0759-1862"},{"first_name":"Gershom","last_name":"Olajire","orcid":"0000-0001-6195-8886"},{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Olumese","orcid":"0009-0003-9194-5500"},{"first_name":"Emeka","last_name":"Idenyi","orcid":"0009-0005-1248-2144"},{"first_name":"Olukayode","last_name":"Oluwafemi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Timothy","last_name":"Oluwasola","orcid":"0000-0001-9904-7316"},{"first_name":"Ayodeji","last_name":"Salami","orcid":"0000-0002-6218-8315"},{"first_name":"Olaposi","last_name":"Omotuyi","orcid":"0000-0002-5473-745X"},{"first_name":"Gabriel","last_name":"Ogun","orcid":"0000-0001-8535-3240"},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Gbadegesin","orcid":"0000-0002-3393-5449"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cervical Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bbz8353xwh","title":"NMR-based Metabolomics Analysis Predict Response to Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy for Triple Negative Breast Cancer","description":"Raw data of the \"NMR-based Metabolomics Analysis Predict Response to Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy for Triple Negative Breast Cancer\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-05T06:30:33.121Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bbz8353xwh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bbz8353xwh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jinping","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Nuclear Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fsjz6gf4xv","title":"Predict Response to Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy for Triple Negative Breast Cancer using Nuclear magnetic resonance-based Urine Metabolomics and Self-organizing maps","description":"Raw data from urine samples of \"Predict Response to Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy for Triple Negative Breast Cancer using Nuclear magnetic resonance-based Urine Metabolomics and Self-organizing maps\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-05T06:30:32.769Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fsjz6gf4xv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fsjz6gf4xv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jinping","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Nuclear Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wr8xrcghmh","title":"Trends in Non-squamous cervical cancer prevalence, histological type, stage, age, and human papillomavirus type in southwest China from 2009 to 2023: A Large Retrospective Study","description":"To describe the trends in non-squamous cervical cancer (Non-SCC) prevalence, histological type, stage, age, and human papillomavirus type in southwest China from 2009 to 2024.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-10T14:59:48.178Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wr8xrcghmh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wr8xrcghmh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Liang","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Epidemiology of Cervical Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"75vdm7phv9","title":"INCAGI004","description":"Randomized controlled trial comparing two neoadjuvant chemotherapy regimes for locally advanced rectal cancer. Stage II and III rectal cancer patients were randomized to receive neoCRT with either capecitabine (Group 1) or 5-Fu and leucovorin (Group 2) concomitant to long course radiotherapy. Clinical downstaging was accessed using MRI 6-8 weeks after treatment. EORTCs QLQ C30 and CR38 were applied before treatment (T0), after neoCRT (T1), after rectal resection (T2), early after adjuvant chemotherapy (T3), and one year after end of treatment or stoma closure (T4). Wexner scale was used for continence evaluation at T4. A C30SummaryScore (Geisinger et cols) was calculated to compare QOL results. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-27T12:49:15.767Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/75vdm7phv9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/75vdm7phv9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rodrigo Otavio","last_name":"Araujo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Radiation Therapy","Chemotherapy","Rectal Cancer","Surgical Oncology","Quality of Life","Randomized Clinical Trial"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"h2th44bw8z","title":"MUC1, MUC4, Stn, TN dataset in cervical cancer ","description":"The photomicrographs are images of immunohistochemically stained cervical cancer and lymph node metastases. The specimens were prospectively collected from patients enrolled in the Gynecologic Oncology Group (GOG) clinical trial GOG0221: Glycoprotein and glycan profiling in patients with locally advanced cervical cancer (Stage IB2, IIA > 4 cm, IIB to IVA) undergoing pelvic and para-aortic (abdominal) lymphadenectomy (clinicaltrials.gov:NCT00460356). Sections of the specimens immunohistochemically stained for mucin 1 and 4 proteins (MUC1 and MUC4, respectively) and Sialyl Tn (STn) and Tn antigens were reviewed by experienced gynecologic pathologists and areas of interest for photomicrography were selected by the first two authors of this brief (R.R. and R.C.). Representative images for all immunohistochemical stains showing their expression patterns as well as positive and negative controls were shown and described.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-31T10:07:03.819Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h2th44bw8z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h2th44bw8z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"rajani","last_name":"rai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Doris","last_name":"Benbrook","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachel","last_name":"Conrad","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cervical Cancer","Biomarker Discovery"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8t7pc9kfzp","title":"Supplemental Sentinel Lymph Node Decision Curve Data ","description":"This data is meant to accompany the manuscript published in the Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology, titled \"Sentinel lymph node biopsy status improves adjuvant therapy decision-making in patients with clinical stage IIB/C melanoma: a population-based analysis\". This supplemental data contains the multivariable logistic regression models developed to predict 5-year melanoma specific death with and without sentinel lymph node status, and a supplemental table demonstrating the net benefit provided by sentinel lymph node status at each risk threshold probability for melanoma-specific death among the entire study cohort. Figures include: \n1) Receiver operating curves of models estimating risk for 5-year melanoma-specific death among patients with stage IIB/C cutaneous melanoma with a) thickness and ulceration alone and b) incorporating sentinel lymph node status.  \n2) Histogram demonstrating distribution of predicted risk among patients with stage IIB/C cutaneous melanoma based upon the Clinicopathologic-Sentinel Lymph Node model. \n3) Decision curve analysis demonstrating added net benefit with inclusion of sentinel lymph node status in estimating risk for 5-year melanoma-specific death compared to a model including thickness and ulceration alone among stage IIB/C cutaneous melanoma patients a) <65 and b) ≥65 years of age. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-11-18T22:01:24.471Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8t7pc9kfzp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8t7pc9kfzp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Cimarron","last_name":"Sharon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Richard","last_name":"Straker","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Phyllis","last_name":"Gimotty","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Emily","last_name":"Chu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tara","last_name":"Mitchell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Miura","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Marchetti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Edmund","last_name":"Bartlett","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Giorgos","last_name":"Karakousis","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"SPORE in Skin Cancer","grant_id":"P50 CA 261608"}],"categories":["Decision Analysis","Melanoma Sentinel Node","Melanoma","Melanoma Surgery","Adjuvant Therapy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"795gdkzjzg","title":"Neoadjuvant chemotherapy first and second breast cancer","description":"Neoadjuvant chemotherapy first and second breast cancer","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-05T13:30:24.352Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/795gdkzjzg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/795gdkzjzg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chengshi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Prognosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2btzwwvyy5","title":"Molecular and cellular composition changes after neoadjuvant letrozole and palbociclib in early luminal breast cancer","description":"The NeoPAL trial compares neoadjuvant letrozole-palbociclib (LP) with chemotherapy (CT) in 103 patients with high-risk early luminal breast cancer. At surgery, the Nanostring BC360™ proliferation score and Ki67 expression are reduced in both arms, together with upregulation of immune-related signatures. Overall, there is very little difference in the changes observed with LP as compared to CT, even in signatures related to response to estrogen - CDK4/6 manipulation. Deconvolution of bulk RNA-seq data reveals high content at baseline in cancer cells, immunosuppressive cancer-associated fibroblasts, FOXP3+ CD4+ regulatory T lymphocytes and TREM2+ macrophages. In contrast, myoepithelial cells, normal-like fibroblasts, FOLR2+ macrophages and SELL+ CD4+ T lymphocytes accumulate after treatment in both arms. A low-ROR score is observed at surgery in 63.3% and 43.5% of patients in LP and CT arms, respectively. No 3-year breast cancer-specific survival event is observed in these patients. These data provide a rationale for chemotherapy-sparing trials in this setting","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-11T21:48:42.289Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2btzwwvyy5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2btzwwvyy5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Paul","last_name":"COTTU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cyclin-Dependent Kinase","Estrogen-Receptor-Positive Breast Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":129092,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":174,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":129092,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":128494,"restricted":69,"embargoed":529},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2157},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":645},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":467},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":428},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":418},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":404},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":373},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":327},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":255},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":216}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":126467},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1907},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":129},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":54},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":512},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":406},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":359},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":339},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":305}]}}